
==============================================================================
T1  The correct condition is F/r unbounded, not F unbounded
==============================================================================
D = F - r F' = -r^2 (F/r)', so F/r = Int_r^inf D/r'^2 dr'.
Flat means D = (v_f^2/GM) r, and Int^inf dr/r diverges: |F|/r -> inf.
   F = 1 + 3 r^0.5            min D        +2.5   dlnD/dlnr +0.4967   dlnv/dlnr -0.2517   F/r    0.0301   (unbounded, F/r -> 0)
   F = 1 + 3 r^0.9            min D        +1.3   dlnD/dlnr +0.8992   dlnv/dlnr -0.0504   F/r      1.19   (unbounded, F/r -> 0)
   F = 1 + 3 r                min D          +1   dlnD/dlnr -0.0000   dlnv/dlnr -0.5000   F/r         3   (F/r -> const: PURE GAUGE, D = 1 exactly)
   F = 1 + 3 r^1.1            min D       -7535   dlnD/dlnr +1.1001   dlnv/dlnr +0.0501   F/r      7.54   (F/r -> inf but D < 0: gravity REVERSES)
   F = 1 + 3 r ln(1e4/r)      min D          +4   dlnD/dlnr +1.0000   dlnv/dlnr -0.0000   F/r    0.0001   (the unique exactly-flat form)
   F = 4.0 (bounded)          min D          +4   dlnD/dlnr +0.0000   dlnv/dlnr -0.5000   F/r    0.0004   (bounded: exactly Keplerian)

   Read the F = 1 + 3 r^0.9 row: F IS unbounded and the curve is still
   declining (dlnv/dlnr = -0.05, not 0).  Read the F = 1 + 3 r row: D = 1
   exactly, a pure gauge shift of Phi.  Only the r ln r form is flat.
   T2 gauge check: adding c r to F changes D by at most 3.01e-07 over the whole range.

==============================================================================
T3  The second, independent no-go: linearity fixes the BTFR slope at 2
==============================================================================
If F does not depend on rho, Phi[rho] is a LINEAR functional of rho, so
doubling the mass doubles the potential and v_f^2 scales as M.  M = v_f^2 x
const is BTFR slope 2.  The observed slope is 3.85 +/- 0.09.  MOND gets 4
only because g = sqrt(G M a0)/r has the coefficient scaling as M^(1/2),
which no linear kernel can produce.
   c independent of M (any linear kernel)   BTFR slope 2.000  (expected 2.0)
   c proportional to M^-1/2 (MOND)          BTFR slope 4.000  (expected 4.0)

   The q field is the only nonlinearity available, and q in [0,1) SATURATES
   in the outskirts -- exactly where the flat part lives -- so the kernel
   becomes linear again there.  The previous lane measured slope 2.88 with
   the modification on and 2.07 with it off; 2 and 4 bracket that, and the
   2.88 is the transition region, not a solution.

==============================================================================
Why R1 (D > 0) and R2 (D proportional to r) exclude each other
==============================================================================
For F = 1 + a G(qbar(r)) H(r),
   D = 1 + a G (H - r H')  -  a r G'(qbar) qbar'(r) H
The last term is strictly NEGATIVE when the modification grows in voids
(G' > 0) and paths get more void-like outwards (qbar' > 0).  Raising a to
lengthen the flat part raises the negative term by the same factor.
   a=0.3   p=0    (UNGATED)  min D over 1-300 kpc      +1.027   mean dlnD/dlnr over 2-20 R_d +0.053
   a=0.3   p=1    (gated  )  min D over 1-300 kpc     +0.8177   mean dlnD/dlnr over 2-20 R_d +0.052
   a=0.3   p=2    (gated  )  min D over 1-300 kpc     +0.9133   mean dlnD/dlnr over 2-20 R_d +0.020
   a=1     p=0    (UNGATED)  min D over 1-300 kpc      +1.091   mean dlnD/dlnr over 2-20 R_d +0.130
   a=1     p=1    (gated  )  min D over 1-300 kpc     +0.3925   mean dlnD/dlnr over 2-20 R_d +0.154
   a=1     p=2    (gated  )  min D over 1-300 kpc      +0.711   mean dlnD/dlnr over 2-20 R_d +0.063
   a=3     p=0    (UNGATED)  min D over 1-300 kpc      +1.273   mean dlnD/dlnr over 2-20 R_d +0.225
   a=3     p=1    (gated  )  min D over 1-300 kpc     -0.8226   mean dlnD/dlnr over 2-20 R_d +0.357
   a=3     p=2    (gated  )  min D over 1-300 kpc      +0.133   mean dlnD/dlnr over 2-20 R_d +0.167
   a=10    p=0    (UNGATED)  min D over 1-300 kpc      +1.909   mean dlnD/dlnr over 2-20 R_d +0.303
   a=10    p=1    (gated  )  min D over 1-300 kpc      -5.075   mean dlnD/dlnr over 2-20 R_d +0.718
   a=10    p=2    (gated  )  min D over 1-300 kpc       -1.89   mean dlnD/dlnr over 2-20 R_d +0.406

   The p = 0 rows are the SAME H(r) with the gate removed: D stays
   positive and the slope still reaches 1.  Turning the gate on at the
   same a drives min D negative.  An ungated form is not a candidate,
   because without a gate the modification is present in the solar
   system too -- which is what R3 tests.

==============================================================================
T4  Why a gate is mandatory, and therefore why R1 fails
==============================================================================
The unique exactly-flat form is F = 1 + c r ln(r_*/r), whose
D = 1 + c r is POSITIVE everywhere and has dlnD/dlnr -> 1.  So an
UNGATED unbounded F does satisfy R1 and R2.  It dies on R3 instead,
and the arithmetic is one line: c is a universal constant, so the
fractional departure from Newton is c r at EVERY radius.
   ISL bound 1e-11 at 1 AU  =>  c <= 2.063e-03 /kpc  =>  D - 1 <= 2.063e-02 at 10 kpc, 6.188e-02 at 30 kpc
   ISL bound 1e-08 at 1 AU  =>  c <= 2.063e+00 /kpc  =>  D - 1 <= 2.063e+01 at 10 kpc, 6.188e+01 at 30 kpc
   SPARC TRAIN demands D at the last measured point: 5/50/95 pct = 1.90 / 4.64 / 9.55
   The ungated form is short by a factor 225 at 10 kpc. Hence a gate,
   hence a rising qbar, hence the strictly negative gate term in D, hence R1.
   For comparison: MOND evades this with an ACCELERATION gate whose
   solar-system suppression is 1/x for the simple mu (2e-8 at 1 AU) and
   1/(2x^2) for the standard mu (2e-16).  A DENSITY gate cannot do that
   job here, because the density gate must also switch on across the
   rotation curve and it is the switching that subtracts from D.

